113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (The main goal of this project was to explore what interactivity actually looks like when it lives inside a physical object rather than a screen or a button. The brief pushed us to build a sculpture that doesn't just react to one action, but responds to the ongoing relationship between its different parts and to make that feel meaningful to whoever engages with it.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (We wanted something that anyone could pick up and interact with straight away, but that also had enough depth to keep people curious. If someone just places a single cube and watches what happens, they're already part of the piece. If someone connects all four and then deliberately introduces the Black Hole knowing what it does  they've essentially told a small story with their hands. We wanted people to feel like they were making something, not just pressing a button.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 87 0 R
/E (On a more practical level, the project was also a way for us to work through the entire pipeline of interactive media from start to finish: physical making, electronics, data communication, and real-time audiovisual software. Every stage threw up its own problems, and solving them pushed us to build skills across a lot of different areas  fabrication, coding, design, and performance  and then pull it all together into one system that actually worked.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 87 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
/A [410 0 R 0]
/K [3]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 87 0 R
/T (Purpose of the Project)
/E (Purpose of the Project)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Choosing a cosmic theme felt personal before it felt like a concept. Space, stars, and the formation of the universe have always been fascinating,  the stories humans tell about the cosmos, from ancient myths to modern science, are some of the oldest ways we've made sense of the world. The universe is the one thing every culture, every era, and every person has in common. That felt like the right backdrop for a piece about connection and collapse.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (It also made practical sense. A solar system builds up one piece at a time, and every new body changes the gravitational pull on everything else. That maps almost perfectly onto how our resistor network works each new cuboid changes the circuit, shifts the voltage, and alters the whole system. At an abstract level, the physics of our sculpture and the physics of a solar system are telling the same story.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The theme also gave each cuboid a clear visual and sonic identity. The star, Earth, Moon, and Black Hole are immediately recognisable to anyone. They already carry emotional weight warmth, life, mystery, destruction and we could lean into that through TouchDesigner and sound/the music without having to spell it out. The audience already knows what these things mean. Our sculpture just puts that understanding into their hands.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 88 0 R
/T (Why the Space Theme?)
/E (Why the Space Theme?)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
