123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (The Cosmos is an interactive sculpture that invites people to help build and ultimately destroy a solar system. It taps into something ancient: that urge to look up at the night sky and search for meaning. But here, that meaning isn't given to you. You make it yourself, one piece at a time.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Inside a box in a quiet room, four laser-cut cuboids wait to be discovered. Each one stands for something: the Star, the Earth, the Moon, and the Black Hole. On their own, they already feel alive. The Star glows with heat. The Earth seems to breathe. The Moon sits in its own stillness. The Black Hole just waits. Each one feels whole  and yet somehow unfinished.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (When you start connecting them, things begin to change. The Star and Earth together fill the projection with warmth and life. Bring in the Moon, and something clicks three bodies in balance, the system at its most complete. A single piece of music carries through the whole experience, steady and unbroken. But every time a new cuboid is placed, the music dips for a moment, like the universe pausing to adjust. Then it comes back. Until the Black Hole is placed. The moment that fourth cuboid connects, a whirlpool swallows the screen. The light fades. Everything goes dark.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (This piece treats interactivity as something more than entertainment it's about responsibility. You don't just watch the cosmos fall apart. You're the one who ends it. Every choice you make carries real weight within the work.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
