179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (The idea for this project grew out of a genuine fascination with space how stars are born, how solar systems form, and the stories people have told about the night sky for thousands of years. Space felt like the right subject because it belongs to everyone. In the most literal sense, it is universal.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (We were also drawn to the fact that a solar system already has a story built into it: it begins, it grows, and eventually it ends. That arc of creation and collapse mapped naturally onto the modular structure of the assignment, where each new cuboid changes the whole system. The concept and the technology felt like they were saying the same thing.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 94 0 R
/T (Interactive Art We Looked At)
/E (Interactive Art We Looked At)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Semiconductor - Ruth Jarman & Joe Gerhardt)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 94 0 R
/E (Semiconductor is an audiovisual duo who build installations using real scientific data from space solar wind, electromagnetic signals and turn them into sound and image. What drew us to their work was the way they take something completely invisible and make it something you can actually feel. That was exactly what we were going for, just at a smaller and more hands-on scale. Their work also showed us that cosmic data could be beautiful without losing its scientific honesty.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
184 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
