197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (We also looked at participatory art more broadly work that only becomes complete when someone physically engages with it. This idea sat at the core of our project. Without an audience picking up the cuboids and placing them, nothing happens. People are not watching the solar system  they are building it. And they are the ones who choose to end it.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 96 0 R
/T (The Black Hole as an Ending)
/E (The Black Hole as an Ending)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 96 0 R
/E (We thought carefully about why the Black Hole felt like the right way to close the piece. In 2019, scientists released the first ever photograph of a black hole. In 2022, they captured the one at the centre of our own galaxy. Both felt like extraordinary moments  something that had only ever existed in theory suddenly became real and visible. A black hole is not just destruction; it is also one of the most mysterious and compelling things in the known universe. Asking the audience to place it themselves  knowing it will collapse everything they have built  felt like an honest way to sit with that complexity.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
